<p>Since <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/07/25/i-love-me-some-em.html">I confessed my enjoyment of em dash</a> in my writing last week, I started using them more freely. On a Mac, it&rsquo;s <kbd>Option Shift Hyphen</kbd>, as Sasha would tell you. In Linux, it&rsquo;s a bit more involved: <kbd>Ctrl Shift U</kbd> to bring up a search for Unicode number reference, and then the number for em dash: <kbd>2014</kbd>.</p>
<p>This is all good and well, but what about Emacs, where I write most of my text?</p>
<p>Emacs comes with <code>abbrev-mode</code>, a powerful text-expanding option that automatically converts something like &ldquo;om&rdquo; to &ldquo;org-mode&rdquo;, or even &ldquo;fox&rdquo; to &ldquo;The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog&rdquo; if I wanted to. But the em dash is a bit more tricky. I could, if I wanted, create an abbreviation like &ldquo;em&rdquo; to simply expand to &ldquo;—&rdquo;. But this doesn&rsquo;t make much sense.</p>
<p>When converting org-mode to markdown (as I do when I post something), the built-in solution for Emacs&rsquo; markdown exporter is to convert <code>---</code> (three dashes) to <code>—</code>, em dash — but this presents two annoyances. First, the built-in exporter would convert those to their character reference, as we just saw: <code>&amp;#2014;</code>. I <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/07/27/style-emacs-config-a-semantic.html">explained this more in-depth</a> previously. Second, even after I fixed this issue by using pandoc, I still see <code>---</code> in org-mode, not —. This looks like the kind of thing <code>abbrev-mode</code> was made for, right?</p>
<p>But it&rsquo;s a bit more complicated. <code>abbrev-mode</code> triggers only when you type a space after a letter. So, &ldquo;em&rdquo; to — would work, but - to — would not, because a dash is not part of a word<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The solution was simpler than I thought: <code>expand-abbrev</code>, which &ldquo;Expands the abbrev before point, if there is an abbrev there. Effective when explicitly called even when &lsquo;abbrev-mode&rsquo; is nil.&rdquo; In other words, it calls expand-abbrev without needing to trigger it the usual way. We simply call the abbrev replacement directly.</p>
<p>Now whenever I want an em dash, I type <code>---</code> first, and then I make sure the marker is right after the last dash, and call <code>expand-abbrev</code>, which in my config is tied to <kbd>C-x &lsquo;</kbd>. I then get an em dash in Emacs, and the exporter in Emacs (or pandoc, in my case) leaves it alone.</p>
